import{_ as o,o as t,c as a,a0 as s}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const n="/pi-extensions/assets/tool-headers-before._oPDwGJ2.png",i="/pi-extensions/assets/tool-headers.CUV7t8GA.png",u=JSON.parse('{"title":"tool-headers extension","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"extensions/tool-headers.md","filePath":"extensions/tool-headers.md","lastUpdated":1792395432000}'),r={name:"extensions/tool-headers.md"};function d(l,e,h,c,p,g){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="tool-headers-extension" tabindex="-1">tool-headers extension <a class="header-anchor" href="#tool-headers-extension" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;tool-headers extension&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Every Tool row sits in the same colored block, and the block says nothing about which tool it holds. A <code>bash</code> and a <code>powershell</code> row print only <code>$ &lt;command&gt;</code>; a <code>codemode</code> row prints a JSON blob; a <code>bash</code> and a <code>bash_bg</code> row are identical in shape. This extension draws one line above each row&#39;s call line - the <strong>Tool row header</strong>: the tool name and its Origin tag, set in a rule - so the tool and the unit that supplied it are told at a glance.</p><h2 id="screenshot" tabindex="-1">Screenshot <a class="header-anchor" href="#screenshot" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Screenshot&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The same session, once without the extension and once with it: the only difference is the header line above each call line. The <code>note_search</code> row in both images is a capture-only fixture extension that stands in for an extension&#39;s row: it registers one tool with fixed arguments and fixed output, so the pair is byte-stable, while a real <code>bash_bg</code> row would carry a job id, pid, and log path that never are.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Without tool-headers</th><th>With tool-headers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><img src="'+n+'" alt="Tool rows without headers"></td><td><img src="'+i+`" alt="Tool rows with headers"></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-header-line" tabindex="-1">The header line <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-header-line" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The header line&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The header is the fieldset legend of the row: the tool name in the theme&#39;s <code>toolTitle</code> (bold), followed by its Origin tag in <code>muted</code> (unbold), set in a rule of <code>─</code> in <code>borderMuted</code>, running to the right edge of the box&#39;s content area. The name and the tag are joined by a <code>·</code> separator in <code>borderMuted</code>, like the rule. The name stays the only bold thing on the line, so the name stays the payload. The line is always exactly one row.</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>(blank)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ── read · built-in ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  read src/auth/session.ts (1-40)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  42 lines</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>(blank)</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="the-origin-tag" tabindex="-1">The Origin tag <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-origin-tag" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Origin tag&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The tag tells the Tool origin - the unit that supplied the tool (ADR 0045). There are three tags:</p><ul><li><code>built-in</code> for pi&#39;s own tools, including the tools of pi&#39;s built-in extensions (<code>codemode</code>, <code>tool_search</code>). Built-ins are tagged on purpose: it keeps &quot;no tag&quot; meaning exactly one thing.</li><li><code>mcp</code> for an MCP server&#39;s tool. The row&#39;s name already carries the server (<code>server/tool</code>), so repeating the server in the tag would say nothing new.</li><li>The extension&#39;s own name for an extension&#39;s tool: <code>bash_bg</code> wears <code>background-jobs</code>, <code>codegraph_search</code> wears <code>codegraph</code>. The tag names the extension, not the package - a git-installed package path carries no package-name segment, and one name per package would say <code>pi-extensions</code> on most rows without ever saying which extension.</li></ul><p>The origin comes from the shared classifier (<code>extensions/shared/tool-origin.ts</code>), which answers the fact, not the rendering: the origin&#39;s kind plus its tag text. The classifier reads pi&#39;s per-tool source metadata, and one name rule that overrides it: pi&#39;s own built-in MCP extension reports its tools as built-in, so only pi&#39;s <code>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code> name rule can say <code>mcp</code> - the same rule the header already uses for its <code>server/tool</code> name. A tool pi does not report - an MCP row in a resumed session before its server connected - gets the name rule and nothing else. No tag is never a guess, and <code>unknown</code> is a word the operator cannot act on, so it is not printed.</p><p>The classifier&#39;s answer for a tool name is read once from <code>pi.getAllTools()</code> and memoized for the life of the extension instance, because the registry call rebuilds its array and <code>renderCall</code> runs on every repaint of every row. A reload re-runs the extension factory and re-reads the registry, so the only stale case is one tool name claimed by two extensions inside one session, which pi resolves by load order anyway. The same classifier serves the <code>/tools</code> list, which keeps its compact convention of no tag for a built-in.</p><h3 id="the-width-ladder" tabindex="-1">The width ladder <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-width-ladder" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The width ladder&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When the width is tight, the line spends the rule first, then the tag, then the name (ADR 0038&#39;s ladder, extended in one order):</p><ol><li>the trailing rule collapses to zero (<code>── &lt;name&gt; · &lt;tag&gt;</code>), the tag still whole;</li><li>the whole tag and its separator disappear together - never a shortened form of the tag - leaving exactly the ADR 0038 line (<code>── &lt;name&gt;</code> filled with <code>─</code>, or <code>── &lt;name&gt;</code> when the rule is gone);</li><li>only if the name alone no longer fits is the name truncated, ending in <code>…</code>.</li></ol><p>Below the point where the tag no longer fits, the line is exactly the one ADR 0038 described.</p><h2 id="scope" tabindex="-1">Scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scope&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The header lands on every Tool row: the built-ins (<code>read</code>, <code>bash</code>, <code>powershell</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>grep</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>ls</code>), this package&#39;s tools (<code>codegraph_*</code>, <code>bash_bg</code>, <code>job_wait</code>, <code>job_status</code>, <code>skill</code>, <code>codemode</code>), <code>tool_search</code>, and MCP tools. The rows that look alike today are exactly the ones that carry the value:</p><ul><li>A <code>bash</code> row and a <code>bash_bg</code> row are identical in shape; the header is what finally tells one from the other. The <code>bash_bg</code> call line stays <code>$ &lt;command&gt;</code> with no background marker (<a href="/pi-extensions/adr/0024-bash-bg-tool-row-shows-the-plain-command-line.html">ADR 0024</a>).</li><li>An MCP row&#39;s header shows <code>server/tool</code>, dropping pi&#39;s <code>mcp__</code> spelling, the same form the row&#39;s call line uses, so the two agree. Every other row shows the tool&#39;s raw name.</li><li>A tool that ships no renderer of its own keeps its stock call line (the shape pi&#39;s fallback draws) and gains the header above it.</li></ul><p>The <code>edit</code> row sets its own shell and draws its diff preview instead of the green box; its header goes above that preview, inside the self shell. Uniformity is the point: a row that skipped the header would be the row the operator misreads.</p><h2 id="how-it-works" tabindex="-1">How it works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How it works&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The mechanism is a <code>pi.registerToolRenderer()</code> resolver that calls <code>next()</code> first and wraps whatever pi or another extension would draw, prepending the header. Wrapping reaches built-in tools, this package&#39;s tools, and MCP tools through one path, and it composes with pi&#39;s own MCP resolver and with <code>bash_bg</code>&#39;s renderer instead of shadowing either (<a href="/pi-extensions/adr/0038-tool-row-headers-wrap-pi-tool-renderers.html">ADR 0038</a>). The resolver never registers a tool: it changes a picture, not an execution.</p><ul><li>A <code>next()</code> of <code>undefined</code> (a tool pi knows no renderer for) yields <code>undefined</code>, leaving the row exactly as pi draws it.</li><li>The wrapper keeps pi&#39;s inner component in per-row state and hands it back as <code>context.lastComponent</code>, so pi&#39;s built-in renderers keep their caches: the <code>edit</code> diff preview, the <code>write</code> highlight, the <code>bash</code> timer and truncation footer all still update, and click-to-expand and inline images keep working.</li><li>The header&#39;s Origin tag comes from the shared classifier (above), and the row&#39;s result block, its shell, and its padding line are untouched: the tag is the only thing the row gains besides the name.</li><li>The renderer serves the interactive transcript and the HTML export alike; the header, tag and all, draws in both.</li></ul><h2 id="always-on" tabindex="-1">Always on <a class="header-anchor" href="#always-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Always on&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The extension is always on, with no setting and no command. It is a rendering opinion in a personal package; the way to opt out is to not load the file.</p>`,26)])])}const m=o(r,[["render",d]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
